Now, factoring the term $3$ from $3$, we have:

\begin{align*} 3 & = -(2A + 3B) \\ \frac{3}{-1} & = \frac{-(2A+3B)}{-1} \\ \frac{3}{-1} & = \frac{-(-2A-3B)}{-1}\\ \frac{3}{-1} & = 2A +3B \\ -3 & = 2A +3B \\ -\frac{3}{1} & = \frac{2A + 3B}{1} \\ \frac{-3}{1} & = \frac{2A + 3B}{1} \\ \frac{-1(-3)}{1} & = \frac{-1(2A + 3B)}{1} \\ \frac{3}{1} & = -(2A + 3B) \end{align*}

Since $3 = -(2A + 3B)$, we can replace $3$ with $-(2A+3B)$ in the previous expression $-2A = 3B$.

\begin{align*} -2A & = -(2A + 3B) \\ 0 & = -(2A) - (2A) - 3B \\ 0 & = -4A - 3B \\ 0 & = (-4A) - 3B \\ 0 & = -4A - 3B \end{align*}

Now we have the equation $0 = -4A - 3B$. To isolate $-4A$, we move $-3B$ to the other side.

\begin{align*} 0 & = -4A - 3B \\ 0 + 3B & = -4A - 3B + 3B \\ 3B & = -4A - 3B + 3B \\ 3B & = -4A \\ \end{align*}

To find the value of A, we multiply both sides by $-\frac{1}{4}$ to get the result.
